it's great to welcome to the program  today Mara vishton doll who's the former  China correspondent for science and also  author of the scientist and the spy a  true story of China the FBI and  industrial espionage so great to talk to  you today mara thanks again for having  me on the show  so let's start with industrial espionage  many people not familiar with this or  may have an idea of what it means Donald  Trump notably talking about it  typically in reference to China China  treating us unfairly China stealing  intellectual property what's the big  picture of industrial espionage is it  typically involving in intellectual  property what are the sort of different  types that maybe exist yes so going  after industrial espionage from China  has been a huge priority of the Trump  administration it is one of the sensible  justifications for the trade war that he  started with China but being focus on it  actually goes back over a decade to the  Obama administration the FBI made  fighting industrial espionage a top  priority and started taking on  investigations involving technologies  like the whitener in Oreo cookies wind  turbines and even genetically modified  corn which is at the heart of my book  and on that note I mean oh so yeah if we  think of the whitener in Oreo cookies  for example doesn't sound like there are  major national security implications  necessarily but when it comes to  industrial espionage how frequently are  classified technologies involved how  frequently are government contractors or  programs and research involved there  have been cases involving national  security technologies but the government  the US government is basically been  arguing that an attack on our US  corporations and an attack on our  economic security is an attack on  national security so it's been this kind  of expansive  reimagining of what national security  means as it has become a priority when  you talk about industrial espionage is  the motivation typically a motivation of  getting an advantage when it comes to  the ability to earn a profit I mean is  that national security instances aside  is that mostly what is at issue here  trying to earn a profit by getting some  kind of technology technique product or  otherwise an element of something from  another company sure yeah Chinese  developing very rapidly or I was  developing I should say before the start  of the global pandemic yeah and there  are a lot of companies who are you know  really just interested in in short term  getting ahead at all costs  stealing technology is the most  expedient way to do that  but you know I wanted to draw attention  to whether this is the best use of our  resources  the policies that the Trump  administration put in place led to the  deterioration of us-china relations  which is now absolutely critical now  we're seeing with the pandemic that  cooperation could be essential to  fighting it and all of this back and  forth over industrial espionage really  led to the decline of of that  relationship so not that an eye for an  eye is the right or the wrong thing but  just so that we have all the information  how frequently is the u.s. doing the  same thing elsewhere the u.s. stance is  that we don't spy for the benefit  specific benefit of our private  corporations there are some people who  take issue with that who point to small  examples of it could have happened here  or there but you know in general the  u.s. is at a point in our development  where we don't need to there were  certainly points in our history where we  did take a lot of technologies even  early America there was quite a lot that  was stolen from England for example but  China's now at that stage where it is  rapidly developing and  where the you know the US has a lot of  desirable technologies I think that the  question is should an ear of corn be  treated like a national security secret  yes so let's get into that story I mean  describe the story that you tell and  highlight in the book the scientist and  the spy so I look at the case of Robert  Moll who was a Chinese born engineer who  is found in an Iowa cornfield in 2011 he  was initially suspected of trespassing  but his appearance there ended up  setting off this 2 year FBI  investigation involving surveillance  planes Airport busts and the use of a  FISA warrant which is intended to be  reserved for extreme national security  threats and what was he trying to  accomplish in that cornfield well it was  a monsanto cornfields the company  treated the next generation a hybrid  corn that was growing there as trade  secrets robert mull worked for a beijing  agricultural company that wanted to  steal that corn at one point they  actually tried to send it back to China  in microwave popcorn bags and so they  had this kind of harebrained scheme and  the FBI meanwhile decided to track them  over years bugging their cars chasing  them across the Midwest and so you had  this kind of absurd cat-and-mouse chase  that in the end was a massive use of  taxpayer taxpayer resources yes so  there's a there's so much to talk about  with regard to this I mean what you  mentioned the question of is this a good  use of FBI resources which are  taxpayer-funded  that's one debate to be had the other  question is is this another example of  corporate power and the degree to which  corporations dictate law enforcement  priorities whether you want to talk  about private prisons existing  influences  the judicial system and how the judicial  system functions should that be the case  I would argue no is that not another  underlying story here which is that  because of what who Monsanto is they  have a lot of say in what the FBI  doesn't doesn't work on oh definitely  you know so I retraced Robert most steps  i retrace the steps of the FBI agent who  is tailing him there at one point there  were seventy some agents working on the  case of dozens of people in multiple  multiple states just a massive use of  resources and you know as I was  traveling around the Midwest talking to  farmers about this case there were many  people said to me look you know Monsanto  and DuPont pioneer another company in  this in this case they have massively  consolidated over the past few years  they eat to the point where the price of  seed has gone up for many many farmers  and when you look at what the  impediments to innovation and  agriculture are that corporate  consolidation is a very big one now on  the other side of this you know it's  very easy to say hey listen there's  intellectual property here and the  intellectual property is being violated  or threatened you've got a guy standing  in a cornfield that's it's sort of  pretty cut and dry aside from whether  the FBI should be involved there only  local police or whatever that's one  question but on the other side of that  is it may be too easy to get  intellectual property protection for  things that one could debate whether  they should or should not be subject to  that level of protection from government  intellectual property infrastructure in  the sense that you know when we talk  about what can you trademark what can  you patent etc that's another side to  this story that is you know I do think  there is a big a big place for IP  protection you know I'm a writer I  believe that my work should be protected  I don't want people out there pirating  my book same with my other hat solutely  agree on the other hand you know when  you  look at what should be done to protect  innovation in this country there's also  a big role for antitrust enforcement and  that's something that has really taken a  backseat to other priorities in the past  few years but even in the Obama years  there were several big antitrust actions  that fell apart and one of them involves  Monsanto so even as the Justice  Department was bringing this case on  basically on behalf of Monsanto there  was an antitrust inquiry explored by  another arm of the department that then  fell apart  well that seems to be if you zoom out  that's the trend right which is when it  comes to allowing intellectual property  protection it by and large benefits  large corporations over small  individuals and we see that you know in  myspace we see it on platforms like  YouTube and others where the benefit of  the doubt by default goes to the large  media company and not the small producer  etc you bring up antitrust which is an  opportunity to regulate and control some  of these large corporations but again  they're it's not nearly enough used as  you're sort of pointing out so is that  the overarching story or what what is  really the overarching story here when  it comes to industrial espionage in your  mind well ultimately I was drawn to this  story because it was such a good story  it had all these twists and turns and  these interesting characters nobody was  really fully all good or all bad and  just very kind of complex and I saw that  I could structure it like a thriller but  at the same time it also tapped into all  these other themes of corporate control  of the expansion of what constitutes  national security of the FBI turning  itself from a law enforcement agency  into a counterintelligence agency which  has also played a big role in in the  expansion of what constitutes an  investigation and and so this by looking  at this one small case I could get at  all of these other issues so if we zoom  out from this case a little bit can you  give us some other types of industrial  espionage that maybe are less obvious  than the  the case we're talking about corn and  Monsanto seeds and and some of those  others that are under that umbrella what  else is included under this umbrella of  industrial espionage more broadly Oh  pharmaceuticals the insulation that's  used in walls any lot of industrial  compounds and chemicals and you know  basically if you look at the major  corporations in America and what they're  manufacturing many of those products  have been the subject of these  industrial espionage  Kait fractions this stuff can be life  and death in some cases right I mean now  when we're talking about n95 masks and  masks that are at issue when it comes to  health care workers that are treating  coronavirus patients and you know you  start hearing about rotten masks or  faulty masks masks that are called and  95 but aren't really I mean this is all  wrapped up with some of this  intellectual property is it not right  well yeah counterfeiting is a big issue  as well and I've just been looking into  that for another story I'm certainly a  big concern and that is really about the  concern over PPE and masks and where  they're coming from that's really about  global supply chain issues and that's  certainly been complicated by the trade  war that the Trump has started with  China and you know also just by the  general decline in us-china relations so  over the past few the few weeks things  have really reached a new low with China  and precisely at the moment when we need  we most need to be cooperating on this  issue so last thing I want to touch on  is as far as the trade war in the  inclusion of again trying to stop  Chinese spying and theft of intellectual  property Donald Trump very loudly said  that China is doing this he said the  trade agree that not Trade Agreement but  the trade the tariffs and the larger  package that he put in place was going  to solve that and he even recently  offhandedly said that this is something  that he has now fixed through his policy  what's the  truth of that is the policy that Trump  put in place including tariffs actually  solving this issue it certainly hasn't  it certainly hasn't helped us in terms  of sourcing medical supplies from China  because there are still some tariffs in  place but just more generally the he has  pushed us away from China things were  already bad when he came into office but  you know in by using phrases like  Chinese virus and in kind of perpetually  pointing fingers encouraging China to do  the same which they have we've reached a  really a new low point and in us-china  relations what would be a policy that  would actually work constructively to  resolve the issue of industrial  espionage between the US and China well  whatever we do we need to put in place a  policy that doesn't make researchers  feel targeted so that's another piece of  the puzzle is that there is this  perception among many Chinese American  and Chinese scientists they are being  singled out because of their ethnicity  and some of them maybe have committed  infractions but there are also cases  where people have been arrested who  turned out to be innocent and so that's  ultimately detrimental to the u.s.  innovation infrastructure if we drive  out a lot of the research force and  we're seeing that now as we try to  develop vaccines and try to develop  treatments for clova 19 that many of the  people who could be working on this are  now back in China because they've been  driven out by overzealous investigations  we've been speaking with Mara vishton  dal who is the former China  correspondent for science and also  author of the book the scientist and the  spy a true story of China the FBI and  industrial espionage mara so great  having you on I really appreciate your  time well thank you again

